d and Josh Turek Latest Polls as GOP’s Chances of Losing Iowa Grow", "source": "newsweek.com", "published": "2026-09-25T07:00:00+00:00", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMihwFBVV95cUxOc1hjNTItZTdyNkxnd0tUaWV3c3pESm90cERzdmlkSzI5emRuWXFUakhEZlkyUWdxMFlTOFhuQWZRM1VlWXRjV3Z5eWdBNFBhNWFDbGR0YlBUUXg2WF9ZVmE0eFVoUUNudWktQlFjek9DTF9YZ3pldnRIOEVabzJYTDRjdmRxUUk?oc=5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": 2.54, "prior_sd": 5.34, "poll_margin": 8.95, "poll_sd": 4.63, "poll_weight": 0.57, "fundamentals": {"margin": -0.4, "lean": -4.5, "environment": 4.1, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": 4.5, "sigma_race": 5.2, "sigma_total": 5.65, "baseline_margin": 6.2, "analyst_adjustment": 0.3, "key_factors": ["Sand leads every recent poll by 4 to 12 points, with published averages clustering near D+7 to D+8", "Open seat after Reynolds' retirement removes the incumbent advantage in an R-leaning state", "Prediction markets price Sand at roughly 84-85 percent and most raters call it Lean D", "Iowa's Republican baseline and a close Senate race temper confidence in the polling lead"], "rationale": "Polls from August through early October uniformly show Sand ahead, most by 7 to 12 points, and even Republican-affiliated surveys give him a four-point lead; markets and most raters agree on a Democratic edge. Iowa's Republican lean and a competitive Senate race keep this short of safe territory.", "watch": "If Republicans consolidate behind Lahn or the tight Senate race boosts GOP turnout and ticket-splitting fades, the polling lead could compress quickly.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Kim Reynolds (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Iowa's governorship is open, with Republican Kim Reynolds retiring and not on the ballot. Democrat Rob Sand, who has built a statewide profile through two rounds of 99-county town-hall tours and large crowd events, faces Republican Zach Lahn, with independent Sondra Wilson also running. Polling from August into early October consistently shows Sand ahead, with margins from four points in Republican-affiliated surveys to eleven or twelve elsewhere, and published averages clustering near seven to eight points. Prediction markets put his win chances around 84 to 85 percent, and most race raters call the contest a Democratic lean, though one rates it a tossup.\n\nThe state's Republican lean and a closely contested Senate race add uncertainty, since Sand's advantage rests more on his personal standing than on partisan fundamentals. Turnout patterns and ticket-splitting in the final weeks will help determine whether his crossover support holds through Election Day.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8753, "margin": 5.7}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8859, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8669, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8657, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8472, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8297, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8734, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8592, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.8769, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.8877, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.8875, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.895, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.8938, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.8853, "margin": 6.5}]}